{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789223050,"nonce":"EnvetlfwDN/syzRgNgAF5SK87AM=","title":"Logical not lowers to bitwise not, and bitwise not lowers to negation","message":"hir::UnaryOp has Neg, Not, FNeg. convert_unary_op maps the frontend's\nNot to Not, which every backend emits as bnot (bitwise), and maps BitNot\nand Plus through `_ =\u003e Neg`. So `not 0` is -1, `not 2.5` flips a\nfloat's bits, and `~5` is -5 rather than -6. ZynML's `!` takes the same\npath, so `!b` on a bool is bnot(1), which is nonzero and reads as true.\n\nFix needs a HirType-aware logical not (icmp eq 0 / fcmp eq 0.0 /\nlength-is-zero for strings, producing bool) and a separate BitNot\nvariant, lowered in all four backends.\n\nConformance: unary/operators.py, bool/printing.py (`not True`),\nint/bitwise.py (`~5`).","files":null}]}